you  it's been just under three weeks since I  uploaded my 10,000 subscribers thank you  video now here we are at the momentous  100 thousand milestone a lot of people  have asked for a behind-the-scenes look  at how I make my videos before we get  started let me just take a moment to  thank all of you amazing people for  sticking with second thought and  supporting the channel I'm also going to  use this video as a Q&amp;A so if you have a  question that I haven't answered by the  end of the video leave me a comment  below and I'll respond to you as quickly  as I can  now on to the main event every second  thought video starts as a simple idea  when something interests me I write it  down on my ever expanding list of video  topics at any given time I have about  two or three months worth of videos to  choose from once I pick the topic I want  to use it's time to research it's  amazing how many scholarly resources you  can find with just a simple Google  search scientific journals and study  reports are my favorite resources  because they provide raw data without  imparting significant bias  I'll take note of the source then  synthesize the information into short  notes before moving on to the next one  once I have enough notes I'll throw in a  dash of my brilliant second thought  brand humor and draft up my script a  couple read-throughs to make sure it  flows nicely then it's time to record  the audio I'm going to let you in on a  big secret you don't need a fancy  recording studio to get decent quality  audio I want to see my studio here it is  my closet all the fabric helps absorb  unwanted noise which is perfect for  recording clean audio as for hardware I  use a zoom h5 recorder with shotgun mic  attachment to make sure I only capture  my voice without any background noise it  usually takes me about 20 minutes to get  everything recorded to my standards from  there I'll head over to my computer to  edit the audio I'll put a link to PC  part vicar comm if you want to see the  exact parts I used in my build but here  are the big points I 760 700 K  superclocked  gtx 1070 16 gigs ddr4 ram and a 500 gig  solid-state drive for the operating  system family they'll be sweet and  battlefield I'm no sound engineer but  I'm very familiar with Adobe Premiere  which is adequate for simple trimming of  audio files I'll take about an hour to  go through and trim out noticeable  breasts and pauses as well as the sound  of muffled crying when I mess up a  sentence for the 15th time once that's  done I'll export it as a high-quality  mp3 and save it for later now I have two  different ways to make the actual video  and it all depends on how proactive I am  in the days leading up to the upload if  I'm good I'll open my script in  Photoshop side-by-side and draw the  assets I'll need for the video before I  even  open after things more often than not  though I'll just draw as I go in that  case I'll have After Effects open with  Photoshop minimized and when I meet a  new character or drawing I'll pull up  Photoshop draw it export it as a PNG  file and import it into After Effects  for drawing I use to walk them into a  stab lift it's nothing fancy I think I  paid about 80 bucks for it but it gets  the job done and it's never giving me  any trouble from there I'll just sit in  my terribly uncomfortable Walmart chair  and edit for about 10 or 12 hours  straight until the video is done and  that's it it's a lot of hard work but  it's so rewarding to finish a video and  know you did a good job  I'll refine my setup when I'm able and  hopefully I'll be able to put out even  better videos in the future again I  couldn't do this without the support of  all your fantastic people your comments  never fail to brighten my day and it's  incredibly rewarding to see our  community grow the way it has together  we'll make second thought one for the  history books remember to ask any  questions you have down below in the  comments thanks for watching and 